we're going to begin this hour of course  with president biden's vow to make  russian president vladimir putin in his  words pay a price for his invasion of  ukraine mr biden told congress his  administration is committed to punishing  russian leaders for their aggression but  he vowed not to send american troops  into ukraine he also said history would  remember the war as having made russia  weaker and the rest of the world  stronger  putin's latest attack on ukraine was  premeditated  and totally unprovoked  he rejected repeated repeated efforts at  diplomacy  he thought the west and nato wouldn't  respond  he thought he could divide us at home in  this chamber in this nation  he thought he could divide us in europe  as well  but putin was wrong  well that was just one of the many  topics the president addressed last  night we're joined now by republican  senator john thune he's from south  dakota the senate minority web senator  good morning good to see you good  morning dale nice to be with you there  did seem to be a lot of bipartisan  support bipartisan applause certainly  when it came to how the president any  comments he made about ukraine and  russia do republicans for the most part  think he's doing a good job on this uh  they do i mean i think a lot of  republicans would argue that it was they  were reactive at the beginning at the  early stages maybe didn't do enough to  deter it but i i give the administration  credit i think they've done a an  exceptional job of rallying uh our  allies we're seeing a unity that we've  never seen before the president talked  about that last night and when it comes  to  you know putting these punishing  economic sanctions on russia when it  comes to supporting ukrainian people  providing military aid so they can  defend themselves we've seen um a unity  among our nato allies unlike anything  that i've seen in my lifetime and i'm  encouraged by that but that said  president  zielinski has said repeatedly we need  help we need help the vice president was  with us this morning she said no way  will there be boots on the ground we saw  interviews from ukraine a young woman  said it's hell here and when you look at  the images you must feel that too it  looks and feels very much like hell can  you imagine a scenario where we have to  say okay enough enough we have to get  involved  can you see that happening well i mean i  think if this were to spread um there's  yeah looking at the human suffering it's  heartbreaking and i think people can see  visually it's just you know it's in  front of them all the time and i think  that's rallying the american people  and the leadership of somebody like  zielinski is i think inspiring to a lot  of americans as well the question of  course obviously always is you know what  role can the united states play what  role should they play and what are our  interests there our interests are clear  when it comes to nato countries we have  an article 5 requirement obligation to  come in and support them ukraine is not  currently a nato ally  so you know those are hard questions to  answer but i think everything short of  putting boots on the ground we should be  doing and i think the administration  gets that and i think the world  community including countries that  typically aren't you know willing to  come and rally to support something like  this or coming and we're seeing even the  swiss switzerland exactly  even switzerland yeah and you know and  you have sweden and finland now want to  join nato which is something we ought to  admit them immediately but um i just  think that the the world is rallying and  interestingly enough and then this is i  think really encouraging people in  russia  are pushing back speaking out against  against putin and at some point maybe  they get to the point where  they decide they don't want him on the  scene  certainly the world is watching the  president talked about the unity agenda  last night and that's what i want to  talk to you about the unity agenda did  you hear anything in his economics plan  that you said okay i could support that  well i think there are there were  several things i thought that were unity  statements he talked about obviously  making making sure our kids are back in  school and keeping them in school that  was huge that's certainly got a lot of  plows on our side funding the police  something that was very big on our side  you know we talked at the end about  fighting opioids that is a killer in  this country we're seeing so many people  lost every year as a consequence of that  supporting our veterans there were a lot  of things in his speech i think that  were designed to sort of be a unity  agenda there are some differences  obviously and he kind of did what most  presidents do at a state of the union  speech throughout some red meat  the one issue gale that i think is going  to be really critical going forward and  it bears on the situation we're talking  about with respect to ukraine and that's  energy and gas prices which have a  choking effect on a lot of people in  this country particularly low and middle  income americans and russia's a big  producer of oil so we've got it we've  got to step up and and provide  fill that gap when it comes to liquid  fuels yeah there has to be a way to  figure out how do we hurt russia without  hurting us correct the other the other  thing i want to talk about speaking of  unity i have to talk about two of your  colleagues in the senate marjorie taylor  green and lauren bobot who chose  what is believed to be a very  inopportune time i think there's never a  good time to heckle during the state of  the union but certainly the moment that  they chose to do it i'm curious about  your reaction to their behavior last  night well i don't think there's any  place for that  you know it's a inappropriate state of  the union speech whether you agree with  the president or not it is an  opportunity to show the american people  the respect that the office deserves  well republicans come out publicly and  really say  let them know publicly that was not okay  to do well i will see  how it unfolds today you know typically  the the house um you know will if there  were steps to be taken they would the  house leadership would probably do that  but i think that there'll be plenty of  condemnation from people colleagues and  whatnot  and just a recognition that those types  of antics in a setting like that are  inappropriate and i think i think  everybody would agree with that he  mentioned his supreme court nominee  katanji brown  johnson are you planning to  can you talk about what you're planning  to do in terms of voting for her  confirmation well i think everybody at  least right now is keeping an open mind  um she comes to obviously she's very  qualified i think that we can you know  establish that right away the question  we will be looking at are or questions i  should say judicial philosophy  temperament  you know whether or not she's going to  be somebody who has a a judicial  philosophy that calls for more  intervention um more uh i would say  arguing stepping on the scales when  we're the other one but you did point  out that kachanji brown jackson i  misspoke um  is imminently qualified  so when you say keeping the options open  keeping the options open for what well i  think that you know again you in my in  my view at least the criteria i always  use is judicial philosophy matters to me  i want judges when they get on the court  to be people who are going to apply the  law on the constitution in an impartial  way  be basically somebody who calls balls  and strikes and not somebody who tries  to weigh in with a you know tries to  influence um  with some sort of political agenda uh  you know issues or cases that come  before them so those are the things i  think a lot of us will look at but i do  think that she is going to be somebody  who will get one fair treatment  respectful treatment uh she'll be  thoroughly vetted like all supreme court  justices or potential justices should be  and um so we will make sure that she  gets a fair process okay fair and  respectful yeah that's all that's a good  start yeah okay thank you senator  very good to see you thank you for  getting up early yeah

